# IH35-TMS — OPERATING METHOD (the single reconciled law; ends the module-vs-class conflict)
**Owner-locked 2026-08-03. This supersedes the standalone "module-first" reading AND the standalone "class-wave"
reading by SEQUENCING them so neither core value is lost. Sources reconciled: `Rule 21` (no partial-wave amnesia,
finish-the-module bar), `Operating-Doctrine-PERMANENT` (class-waves, modules last), `FULL-SYSTEM-AUDIT-AND-WIRING-
MASTER-PLAN` (module deep-audit → build). If any of those disagree with THIS file, THIS file wins; a coder folds
them into one consistent statement in the governance PR.**

## Why there is no real conflict
- Rule 21's true demand = **"never a partial slice called done"** (Accounting's 6-of-6 that skipped the full bar).
- The Doctrine's true demand = **"fix a shared root cause ONCE, with a ratcheting guard, before calling anything
  finished; modules close last."**
These compose. A ratcheting class-guard on zero-remaining offenders is a *stronger* anti-partial-wave control than a
hand-checked module close. So we drain classes first, then close modules — Rule 21 is satisfied at the close;
the Doctrine is satisfied at the drain.

## THE METHOD (every agent, every session)
1. **MEASURE FIRST — re-bucket all OPEN FAILs by root-cause class** (Cascade, one pass, correct reads + completeness
   discriminator). Output: how many shared classes vs module-unique remainder. The numbers tune the emphasis —
   ≤~20 classes ⇒ class-drain dominates; mostly-unique ⇒ vertical close dominates. Method follows data, not theory.
2. **DRAIN SHARED CLASSES system-wide** — one wave = one root cause + one shared helper + ONE ratcheting guard that
   stays RED until zero instances remain, proven live. Discovery layer order **C → B → A → D → E**. **Money classes
   serial + 3-gate** (CI-green + Neon entity-scoped tie-out + live surface); **mechanical/UI classes parallel.**
   **One wave ahead:** Cascade pre-audits the next class while fixers drain the current.
3. **VERTICAL MODULE CLOSE LAST (Rule 21 / N-of-M)** — after the shared classes are drained, close each module to
   its FULL bar (every leaf · picker/+Create · deep linkage · economics · live proof, both entities). This is where
   "X/30 certified" moves. Money modules first (accounting, banking, settlements, fuel, dispatch). A module is
   **never** certified while a shared class it participates in is still open — that is false green, forbidden.

## Execution & speed — pipeline + caps (amendment 2026-08-03; Cursor efficiency review, Claude-ratified)
1. **MEASURE is timeboxed.** First re-bucket pass ≤ ~4–6h wall-clock (or top-N OPEN FAILs by severity: money →
   false-green → dead chrome first). It publishes: class count + a ranked wave queue (≤~15 waves) + the
   module-unique remainder. The cap bounds **breadth, never rigor** — every published class still carries its
   completeness discriminator. Re-bucketing then continues **one wave ahead**; nothing outside the queue blocks Wave 1.
2. **Publish Wave 1 early.** The moment ≥1 shared class is proven (instance list + guard spec), fixers start it. Do
   NOT wait for a complete inventory — "finish the whole re-bucket, then build" is the backlog trap moved earlier.
3. **Cascade owns the instance list; the fixer owns the fix.** Each wave card = the proven instance list (paths +
   entities + completeness notes + hotfile overlap). The coder writes one shared helper + one ratcheting guard + the
   PRs against that list — it does NOT re-grep the monorepo. **The guard fails on any remaining instance — LISTED OR
   AUTO-DISCOVERED** — so an incomplete list can never ship as "drained." (This is what makes the timebox safe.)
4. **Concurrency caps (mechanical).** Money = **1 serial stream** (Claude); never two money classes at once.
   Mechanical = **≤3 parallel** Cursor waves, only when the classes don't share hotfiles (Cascade flags overlap on
   the card). **One open PR per hotfile class** (scoreboard / sidebar / palette baselines already taught this).
5. **"Class drained" = shippable, not a re-audit.** Drained = ratcheting guard GREEN on zero offenders in CI (guard
   mutation-proven BOTH directions) + GUARD live spot-check on a sample, BOTH entities. **For money / false-green
   classes the sample MUST include a money-critical instance** (precedent: silent-success shipped a false court
   exhibit — never sample only the harmless one). Full per-surface click-through belongs to the VERTICAL MODULE
   CLOSE, not the wave.
   **Every wave card carries a `DRAIN_PROOF:` line** so "drained" is unambiguous at merge time:
   `DRAIN_PROOF: guard-green · GUARD sample = <path/entity/JE-or-row> · money-critical: yes|no`.
   For a money/false-green class, `money-critical: yes` and the sample path MUST be a money-bearing instance.
6. **One truth = the ledger.** The scoreboard renders the ledger; the 13-gate tally names the weakest column = the
   next wave. If the scoreboard and the wave queue disagree, the scoreboard is stale — fix the ledger row, not the story.
7. **Governance PR is speed infrastructure — land it before any Wave-2 product work.** Until the holds /
   `JORGE-APPROVED` are purged and the always-read set is identical for all agents, every session burns time
   re-litigating merge law.

## Governance (already owner-locked 2026-08-03 — no confusion)
NO HOLDS. NO `JORGE-APPROVED`. Claude and all coders have **FULL Neon access** and **merge on green** in every lane;
they apply migrations + flip flags themselves. Owner steers by **decision in chat** only. Safeguard = **PROOF, not
approval**: additive/idempotent + guard + tests → verify live → apply on Neon → **GUARD verifies live AFTER**. WORM /
void-not-delete · no TMS→QBO write-back · posting flags OFF until the owner says "turn it on" in chat.

## Lanes (anti-collision)
Cascade = auditor (buckets by class, writes findings, pre-audits one wave ahead, never fixes/certifies). Cursor =
mechanical/UI + non-financial + scoreboard. Claude Coder = financial cluster. GUARD = independent live verify AFTER
merge. They never edit each other's files or ledger columns; `git pull --ff-only` before every write.
